module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Subfield.Basic
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Basis.Defs
public import Mathlib.Algebra.CharP.Basic

/-! # Frobenius basis span of a compositum

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 07P4, proof of (2), the displayed
coefficient presentations, generalized from power bases to any finite basis.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-frobenius-basis-compositum)
-/

@[expose] public section
set_option autoImplicit false
universe u v

namespace FieldTheory

/-- The Frobenius image of a finite basis spans the compositum over any subfield
containing the base Frobenius image. This span need not be direct.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 07P4 (`lemma-intersection-subfields`),
proof of (2), the displayed coefficient presentations. This is their span assertion
with an arbitrary finite basis instead of a power basis; no family or intersection
hypothesis is involved.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-frobenius-basis-compositum)

Proof:
Put b_i = e_i^p. Expanding any y in the finite K-basis e and applying Frobenius
shows that L^p is the K^p-span of b. In particular 1 and every product b_i b_j
have expansions in b with coefficients in K^p. Let W be the S-span of b in L.
These expansions show that W contains 1 and is closed under multiplication:
expand each product of two linear combinations using the expansions of b_i b_j.
It contains the image of S and L^p. It is generated by finitely many b_i as an
S-vector space. Multiplication by a nonzero w in W is an injective S-linear
endomorphism of W. Map an S-basis of W by this injection: its image is an independent
family of dim W elements, hence a basis, so the endomorphism is surjective. Its
preimage of 1 gives w's inverse. Thus W is a subfield containing the generators
of the compositum. Conversely every subfield containing those generators contains
each b_i and every S-linear combination of the b_i. The two containments identify
W with the subfield closure, proving the asserted coefficient description.
No independence over S is used, including when S contains a p-th power basis vector. -/
theorem frobenius_basis_compositum
    (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (K : Type u) [Field K] [CharP K p]
    (L : Type v) [Field L] [CharP L p] [Algebra K L]
    {n : ℕ} (e : Module.Basis (Fin n) K L)
    (S : Subfield K) (hS : Set.range (fun y : K ↦ y ^ p) ⊆ (S : Set K))
    (x : L) :
    x ∈ Subfield.closure (Set.range (fun y : L ↦ y ^ p) ∪
      algebraMap K L '' (S : Set K)) ↔
      ∃ c : Fin n → K, (∀ i, c i ∈ S) ∧
        x = ∑ i, algebraMap K L (c i) * (e i) ^ p := by sorry

end FieldTheory
